</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:34:37.433000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>well that's why I say it's subtle</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:34:43.485000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>for now just do it my way</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="12">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:03.620000</ts>
  <user>Mckay</user>
  <text>I know your way works I don't understand why</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:04.660000</ts>
  <user>Neisha</user>
  <text>Any default arguments specified in a function are computed when the function is declared</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="12">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:15.810000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>which only happens _once_</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:19.474000</ts>
  <user>Neisha</user>
  <text>as opposed to when the function is invoked</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="12">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:22.276000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>how can i get maximum occuring thing from list ?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="12">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:26.857000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>which happens lots of times</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:28.309000</ts>
  <user>Andriana</user>
  <text>is it bad that i just found out about how modules work?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:45.776000</ts>
  <user>Neisha</user>
  <text>@Cortax Well your name indicates you're just starting so there's no shame in learning something for the first time</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:35:54.206000</ts>
  <user>Mckay</user>
  <text>So</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:36:08.771000</ts>
  <user>Mckay</user>
  <text>`arg = None` vs `arg = []`</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:36:09.924000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>a very indirect way of demonstrating the same thing: ```py&gt;&gt;&gt; [] == []True&gt;&gt;&gt; [] is []False&gt;&gt;&gt; ```</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:36:14.149000</ts>
  <user>Andriana</user>
  <text>ok cause i keep importing discord and discord commands and didnt understand what i was doing untill now</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="12">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:37:42.503000</ts>
  <user>Mckay</user>
  <text>so hm in one case arg is initialised to a list when function is declared and like everything keeps getting appended to the same list as list is mutable?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:37:58.860000</ts>
  <user>Mckay</user>
  <text>whereas with None it's initialised to nothing so everytime you pass a list to the function you're passing a new object</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:38:00.540000</ts>
  <user>Mckay</user>
  <text>yes?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:38:22.677000</ts>
  <user>Neisha</user>
  <text>And also because `None` is a singleton</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:38:26.933000</ts>
  <user>Neisha</user>
  <text>it's immutable</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="12">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:38:36.940000</ts>
  <user>Mckay</user>
  <text>mm yeah</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:39:07.494000</ts>
  <user>Neisha</user>
  <text>this behavior can be really useful when you're generating functions on the fly though and need to dynamically set a default value for the function</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="10">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:40:44.342000</ts>
  <user>Neisha</user>
  <text>but on the whole any default value should be immutable</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="12">
  <ts>2020-04-01T04:49:21.162000</ts>
  <user>Lucius</user>
  <text>```py&gt;&gt;&gt; def foo(bars=[]):... bars.append('bar')... print(bars)... &gt;&gt;&gt; foo()['bar']&gt;&gt;&gt; foo()['bar bar']&gt;&gt;&gt; foo()['bar bar bar']```yeah you usually don't want this happening</text>
</message>
