  <ts>2020-04-01T11:52:19.673000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>```if __name__ == __main__':``` can someone explain to me what ``` __name__ __main__``` are or where I can learn what they mean?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:53:47.144000</ts>
  <user>Gisselle</user>
  <text>well dunder name is the name attribute of the module you are in. Checking it against dunder main is trying to see if you are executing the module or importing it.</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:54:19.061000</ts>
  <user>Marquavious</user>
  <text>It's a special variable that's set by the interpreter for every file it's the dunder main when you directly execute it</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:54:21.600000</ts>
  <user>Marquavious</user>
  <text>!d __main__</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:54:22.066000</ts>
  <user>Safal</user>
  <text>None</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:55:15.249000</ts>
  <user>Maximilien</user>
  <text>Huh</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:55:44.335000</ts>
  <user>Marquavious</user>
  <text>init would be behind object but you can test it more in #botcommands</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:58:09.137000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>so i do ```pyif __name__ == __main__':``` at the end all the time? when I am messing around with oop?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:58:19.674000</ts>
  <user>Marquavious</user>
  <text>you usually don't need to</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:58:37.229000</ts>
  <user>Dazie</user>
  <text>You need it for multiprocessing</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:58:53.733000</ts>
  <user>Gisselle</user>
  <text>some people will call that an antipattern and tell you never to do it ... however much of the code examples you find in the wild will use it.</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:58:55.707000</ts>
  <user>Marquavious</user>
  <text>only if you want to have different behaviour when you're running it directly as opposed to importing or some other way of running it under your main script</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:59:44.734000</ts>
  <user>Gisselle</user>
  <text>it is also common to see it in unit test files where it invokes pytest or unittest</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T11:59:59.439000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>so for this code is it importing it or executing it```pyfrom kivy.app import Appfrom kivy.uix.widget import Widgetclass PongGame(Widget): passclass PongApp(App): def build(self): return PongGame()if __name__ == __main__': PongApp().run()```</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T12:00:19.932000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>and how did you know what it is doing?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T12:01:04.343000</ts>
  <user>Anabelle</user>
  <text>if you import that file into another file with `import` the `if __name__...` will never execute</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T12:01:15.144000</ts>
  <user>Anabelle</user>
  <text>if you run it by itself it will</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T12:03:43.942000</ts>
  <user>Gisselle</user>
  <text>create a couple of files and put `print(__name__)` as the only line of code in them. Then include one in the other and look at the behavior. ```python#file1.pyprint(__name__)#file2.pyimport file1print(__name__)```then call python on them```python file1.pypython file2.py```</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
  <ts>2020-04-01T12:04:07.991000</ts>
  <user>Marquavious</user>
  <text>say I have a file  myfile.py that looks like this```pythonimport myimportif __name__ == __main__": ...```and then do `python myfile.py`We executed myfile directly so the `__name__` will be `__main__` but we imported myimport so the `__name__` inside that file will not be `__main__` but `myimport`Then if we imported `myfile` the code under if name main would not executed because the file\'s `__main__` would be `myfile`</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="24">
