  <text>Not sure if this is the right place to ask but i have noticed an interesting behavior in numpy and i can't seem to find an explanation. I have a piece of code where i take 25000 images and transform them into arrays using OpenCV. I then create a list of the arrays and their corresponding labels and in the following code snippet i transform the lists into a numpy array in one case and directly populate the numpy array in the other. The end result is two identical arrays. My question here is when i save the two numpy arrays to disk the array X that was created as `numpy.empty()` and then populated takes up roughly 4x as much space as the identical array X1 which started off as a python list and was transformed into a numpy array later. ```py#Python listX1 = []y1 = []#Numpy arrayX = np.empty((len(training_data) IMG_SIZE IMG_SIZE,1))y = np.empty((len(training_data),))#training_data is of shape [100x100 array value]data_counter = 0for features label in training_data: X[data_counter] = np.dstack([features],) y[data_counter] = label data_counter += 1for features label in training_data: X1.append(features) y1.append(label)X1 = np.array(X1).reshape(1 IMG_SIZE IMG_SIZE 1)#save the datanp.save(SAVE_FILE_X X)np.save(SAVE_FILE_X1 X1)print(X.all() == X1.all())#trueimport syssys.getsizeof(X)sys.getsizeof(X1)```Can anyone explain why this happens?</text>
