<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:09:19.034000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>How do i calculate big o?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:11:10.588000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>you don't really calculate it; you figure it out by looking at the code</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:11:30.749000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>huh</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:11:38.950000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>if you see the code has a single loop that goes through the input once that's `O(N)`  proportional to the size of the input</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:12:07.499000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>if there's a nested loop e.g. where the outer goes through each item and then the inner one _also_ goes through each item that's `O(N^2)`</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:12:09.306000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>etc</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:12:22.855000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>huh..</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:12:48.860000</ts>
  <user>Ridgely</user>
  <text>You calculate it by figuring out how many calculation your algorithm needs to go through</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:13:10.401000</ts>
  <user>Kriti</user>
  <text>cacluate figure whatever</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:13:28.057000</ts>
  <user>Ridgely</user>
  <text>Like offby1 said finding an item in a collection is O(n) because you have to go through and check every single item ( worst case )  so you need to do n calculations</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:13:44.409000</ts>
  <user>Ridgely</user>
  <text>There are algorithm to optimize this but generally a for loop is often O(n)</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:13:52.367000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>So a simple function that goes through 3 items to find the average will have a big o of what?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:14:02.161000</ts>
  <user>Ridgely</user>
  <text>It will be O(n)</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:14:05.636000</ts>
  <user>Ridgely</user>
  <text>You have to generalize it</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:14:23.836000</ts>
  <user>Ridgely</user>
  <text>If your collection has 3 items  O(3) 5 items O(5) n items O(n)</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:14:43.137000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>huh..okay</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
  <ts>2020-04-02T03:15:37.516000</ts>
  <user>Kiva</user>
  <text>big O measures asymptotic behaviorit\'s the difference between  what happens if I double the input size  (a) it takes about the same time (b) it takes twice as long (c) it won\'t complete before the heat death of the universe</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="57">
