<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:21:08.861000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>hey fellow humans</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:21:43.601000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>can someone tell me the most basic definition of the self variable</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="88">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:22:03.451000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>@applepy Sure I can give you an example and break it down real quick</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:22:13.329000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>yes please</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:22:31.910000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>So you know where it's used in most methods of classes. So let's make a quick example class so we can easily point to it:</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:22:52.619000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>I know where it can be used</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:23:01.474000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>I just get confused by the definition</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:23:20.069000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>None</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:23:29.083000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>Some code I was writing earlier today</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:24:03.105000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>```pyclass Student: def __init__(self name grade): self.name = name self.grade = grade def introduce(self): print(f"Hi my name is {self.name} and I\'m in the {self.grade} grade!.")```Right</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:24:12.302000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>Okay so say we have:</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:25:09.611000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>```pybilly = Student("Billy 2nd")sally = Student("Sally 1st")```We can then have them call their associated `.introduce()` method.</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:25:23.985000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>Simple we know that bit. But what is actually happening when we do?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:25:25.212000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>mhm</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:25:39.707000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>We can look at it another way instead of `billy.introduce()`</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:25:49.022000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>We can think of it as `Student.introduce(billy)`</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:25:56.960000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>Yes</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:26:07.594000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>`self` refers to the class instance that is being called</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:26:28.627000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>instance?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:26:32.661000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>That is where I get confused</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:26:59.170000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>An instance is an object of the class. So in our case `billy` and `sally` are objects/instances of the Student class</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:27:06.102000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>ahhhhh</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:27:11.330000</ts>
  <user>Attila</user>
  <text>An instance being a specific point in time or specific thing</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
  <ts>2020-04-02T17:27:15.253000</ts>
  <user>Feynman</user>
  <text>That;s what I needed</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="91">
