</message>
<message conversation_id="143">
  <ts>2020-04-03T20:35:16.164000</ts>
  <user>Viana</user>
  <text>and the whole concept of the for loop's counter</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="143">
  <ts>2020-04-03T20:35:17.388000</ts>
  <user>Lukas</user>
  <text>Basically due to how iteration works</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="143">
  <ts>2020-04-03T20:35:28.773000</ts>
  <user>Edwardo</user>
  <text>Dang I gotta do some research about iteration in python</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="143">
  <ts>2020-04-03T20:35:32.462000</ts>
  <user>Lukas</user>
  <text>you remove first element and so the next one gets skipped</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="143">
  <ts>2020-04-03T20:35:46.097000</ts>
  <user>Lukas</user>
  <text>just don’t mutate stuff while iterating over it okay?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="143">
  <ts>2020-04-03T20:36:17.025000</ts>
  <user>Lucius</user>
  <text>going down to the low level if you remove something from the list then the position of all the elements in memory after the one removed will then be shifted back one messing the indexing up</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="143">
  <ts>2020-04-03T20:36:29.650000</ts>
  <user>Lukas</user>
  <text>Haha I got it!</text>
</message>
