  <text>Hi guys ..what is the GIL in python</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:12:32.325000</ts>
  <user>Romero</user>
  <text>Does it mean that python can't multithread</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:13:20.530000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>&lt;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Global_interpreter_lock&gt;</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:13:23.220000</ts>
  <user>Abir</user>
  <text>Global Interpreter Lock. Keeps you bound to one process (I believe). But you can absolutely do that type of thing with `threading` `mulitprocessing` or `async`</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:14:21.715000</ts>
  <user>Abir</user>
  <text>_the GIL is a good thing_</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="163">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:14:30.031000</ts>
  <user>Romero</user>
  <text>Ohhh</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:14:36.879000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>&lt;https://realpython.com/pythongil/&gt; this explains well</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="163">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:14:39.557000</ts>
  <user>Romero</user>
  <text>I'm sorry I'm so new to all these concepts</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="163">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:15:08.201000</ts>
  <user>Romero</user>
  <text>Multithreading means that there can be more than two threads doing two different things at the same time ?</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:15:10.854000</ts>
  <user>Abir</user>
  <text>No need to apologize. I still have trouble with them</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:15:21.757000</ts>
  <user>Romero</user>
  <text>So python can do two different things at the same time</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:15:34.972000</ts>
  <user>Heidy</user>
  <text>&gt; The GIL is a single lock on the interpreter itself which adds a rule that execution of any Python bytecode requires acquiring the interpreter lock. This prevents deadlocks (as there is only one lock) and doesn’t introduce much performance overhead. But it effectively makes any CPUbound Python program singlethreaded.</text>
</message>
<message conversation_id="162">
  <ts>2020-04-04T06:15:39.774000</ts>
  <user>Abir</user>
  <text>I'd suggest reading those pages neon linked. They will explain it really well</text>
</message>
